  "slug": "basis-sbds-diamond-syndrome-eb31",
  "summary": "Four preprints report how autoinhibition, aptamer binding, partial cooperativity and pseudo-helicase motifs govern AAA+ and translocation machinery.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is conformational control of energy-coupled molecular machines, in which ATP binding and hydrolysis, subunit coordination or regulatory domain switching determine function. In the archaeal VAT heterohexamer, reconstituted with motif-dependent partial cooperativity, ATP hydrolysis and substrate unfolding respond differently, indicating that these outputs are not obligately linked. The SecA study places pseudo-helicase motifs in ATP-driven coupling to polypeptide translocation through the SecYEG channel, tested by site-directed mutagenesis. The p97 study shows that a DNA aptamer binding the dynamic p47 adaptor remodels its regulatory architecture through allosteric binding and serves as a chemical biology probe. The TsaP crystal structure reveals autoinhibition of its LysM domain, which PilQ binding relieves to anchor type IV pilus secretins to peptidoglycan. Together, these preprints extend mechanistic detail on how regulatory states gate activity across bacterial, archaeal and p97-family systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
